<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fertile Crescent</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as al-hilāl al-ḵhaṣīb</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fertile Crescent is a crescent-shaped region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> spanning modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, Israel, Jordan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, together with northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a>, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. It is believed to be the first region where settled farming emerged, and it was home to the eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> founder crops, among them emmer wheat, einkorn, barley and lentil, and to four of the five most important domesticated animals. Early civilisations such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> flourished there, and irrigation, writing, the wheel and glass first appeared in the region. The archaeologist James Henry Breasted popularised the term in 1914 and 1916.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The region has no single name in antiquity. James Henry Breasted described it as lying like an army facing south, with one wing along the eastern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the other reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, while the centre has its back against the northern mountains. The boundaries are set by the Syrian Desert to the south, the Anatolian and Armenian highlands to the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> to the east, and the water comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and the Jordan River.</p><p>The area is a bridge between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>, and it kept more biodiversity than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> or North Africa, where Ice Age climate changes pressed ecosystems against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Its great range of elevation produced many edible plants, and many of them are annuals that can self-pollinate and so did not depend on other plants for reproduction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region holds sites of early humans at Tabun and Es Skhul caves, of later hunter-gatherers and of the semi-sedentary Natufians. The western zone around the Jordan and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> gave rise to the first known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> farming settlements, of the Pre-Pottery Neolithic A, dating to around 9,000 BCE and including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobekli-tepe">Göbekli Tepe</a>, Chogha Golan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> the region saw complex societies, early writing and hierarchical states, which earned it the nickname of the cradle of civilization. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> rose and fell in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> basin. Irrigation greatly extended the natural fertility, and the last two millennia have seen cycles of decline and recovery as works fell into disrepair when states were replaced, along with the slow build-up of salt in long-irrigated soils.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Studies of skulls and of genes point to a diverse population in the Fertile Crescent and to migration out of it, westward into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, northward to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and north-eastward to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. On this view, farming spread chiefly through the movement of people, who interbred with the hunter-gatherers they met, rather than through shared knowledge alone.</p><p>Most present-day Europeans derive from at least three ancient populations, one of them the Early European Farmers, descended from Near Eastern migrants. The region was also linguistically varied. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a> generally prevailed in the south and west, alongside Sumerian, a language isolate, while Elamite, Gutian and Kassite were found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and Hattic, Kaskian and Hurro-Urartian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fertile Crescent” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 10,000 BCE Natufians carried parthenocarpic figs from Africa to the south-eastern corner of the <b>Fertile Crescent</b>, and they may have visited Cyprus, which would have meant crossing considerable stretches of open water.</blockquote><a href="/culture/natufians">Natufians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
